{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you’re leading a game of 20 questions\nbut halfway through you forgot the thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5569,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you chose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6569,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have to keep answering yesses and nos\nand hope that you think of something that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10990,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consistent with all your previous questions\nbefore the game is done. Well it could be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16189,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s what the entire universe is doing\nas it creates our reality. I hope it thinks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21850,
    "dDurationMs": 9019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of something good before we run out of questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30869,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists are fairly attached to the idea\nthat the universe is real. We’re fond of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35220,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the realist viewpoint: that there’s an objective\nreality out there that’s entirely independent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40900,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our subjective experience of it. That seems\ninnocent enough, and it served physics awfully"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46080,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well. But take this viewpoint to the extreme\nand we imagine the scientist as this perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52060,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detached observer of the world, capable of\nmonitoring and modeling physical reality without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58060,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever disturbing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59230,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then came the 20th century, and quantum\nmechanics. In quantum mechanics, not only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64200,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does the act of measurement profoundly influence\nthe thing being measured, but it seems we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68460,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t even assign physicality to the world\nbetween measurements. As Niels Bohr put it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74220,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“no phenomenon is a phenomenon, until it\nis an observed phenomenon”. Bohr led the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79650,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge with this observer-dependent view,\nencapsulated in his Copenhagen interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84750,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics, which insisted that\nthe world only became meaningfully defined"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90100,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the act of measurement. Others were\ndesperate to maintain a physical and realist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96130,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, including Albert Einstein himself.\nWe’ve talked about this debate between the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100060,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "founders of quantum theory before, and of\nsome of the supposed resolutions, which I’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104690,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come back to. Today I want to begin a discussion\nof a family of quantum interpretations which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110470,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say that the universe exists not so much in\nphysical particles and quantum fields, nor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115340,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solely in the mind of the observer, but rather\nin the interaction of the two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119820,
    "dDurationMs": 4729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To give a better sense of why we need to go\nto these crazy lengths to explain the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124549,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s review some quantum weirdness. We’ll\nstart with the good ol’ Schrodinger’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129229,
    "dDurationMs": 5361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cat thought experiment, devised by ardent\nrealist Erwin Schrodinger to highlight the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134590,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absurdity of the Copenhagen viewpoints. We\nhave a cat in a closed box that is either"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140910,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "killed or not killed by a random quantum process.\nFor a scientist running the experiment, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146290,
    "dDurationMs": 7279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cat is in a twin state of both alive and dead\nuntil the box is open - a superposition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153569,
    "dDurationMs": 5811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states. Or so quantum mechanics seems to suggest.\nThis seems even more absurd when you add the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159380,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so-called Wigner’s friend extension, where\na second scientist waits on the report from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164630,
    "dDurationMs": 7439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first, rendering both cat and first scientist\ninto a superposition of states. And doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172069,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cat get to observe its own state? Perhaps\nto the cat, the scientists are in a similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177930,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The heart of the problem was a simple question:\nin a world where observation plays a role"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184440,
    "dDurationMs": 6409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in defining reality, how is it possible for\nseparate observers to share a single, consistent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190849,
    "dDurationMs": 3481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality?\nSolutions to this seeming paradox spanned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194330,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spectrum. For example, the Hungarian physicists\nJohn von Neumann and Eugene Wigner doubled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200080,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down on the observer-centric view, in which\nthe act of observation in some way creates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205739,
    "dDurationMs": 6671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality. Others found schemes by which a real,\nphysical universe could be interpreted in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212410,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the behavior of the wavefunction, such as\nde Broglie-Bohm pilot wave theory or objective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218069,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse interpretations. Still others sought\nto explain the observer’s influence as a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223780,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of selection bias - for example, Hugh\nEverett’s many worlds interpretation and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229440,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ideas of quantum decoherence. And then\nwe have Richard Feynmann’s “no interpretation”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235569,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation of quantum mechanics, in which\nhe suggested that his colleagues quit philosophizing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241879,
    "dDurationMs": 1701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and to “shut up and calculate”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243580,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the end, most physicists took that advice.\nThey were eager to put the whole mess behind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249200,
    "dDurationMs": 5752,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them and get on with the job of actually using\nquantum mechanics to invent the modern world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254952,
    "dDurationMs": 6217,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a very select few physicists refused to\nlet it go. One of those was John Archibald"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261169,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler, a prolific physicist whose students\nincluded Feymnann, Everett, Jacob Bekenstein,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267620,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Kip Thorn among others. Wheeler was never\ncompletely satisfied with any of the proposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273800,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum interpretations. Although he started\nout as a pure realist, he came to believe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280050,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the observer must in some way be fundamental."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284099,
    "dDurationMs": 6711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet he couldn’t accept what he saw as\nthe solipsistic view of von Neumann and Wigner,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290810,
    "dDurationMs": 6919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the observer was in a sense the primary\ncausal agent and center of its own reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297729,
    "dDurationMs": 4101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After decades of thought, Wheeler came to\na view that is summarized in his expression"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301830,
    "dDurationMs": 6119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“it from bit.” In his words, “Every\nit — every particle, every field of force,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307949,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even the spacetime continuum itself — derives\nits function, its meaning, its very existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314139,
    "dDurationMs": 6791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entirely… from the apparatus-elicited answers\nto yes or no questions, binary choices, bits.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320930,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, he came to believe that the\nuniverse is fundamentally informational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327289,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler felt that reality must have its primary existence\nnot in the observer nor in the subject of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333050,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observation, but rather in the connection\nbetween the two. One of the thought experiments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337879,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that led Wheeler to his view is his delayed-choice\nexperiment, which is an analogy to the double-slit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345199,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment. Here’s one example of this.\nImagine a single photon hitting a beam splitter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350749,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- a semi-reflective mirror that has a 50-50\nchance of transmitting or reflecting the photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356330,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we add a pair of detectors we see that\neach photon randomly arrives in detector 1"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362080,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or detector 2, revealing whether it was transmitted\nor reflected by the beamsplitter. But remember"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368490,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I said that the quantum world appears to live\nin a state of uncertainty until it’s observed?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373889,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case, quantum mechanics states that\nthe photon is in a state of having been both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transmitted and reflected until we make an\nobservation to check which happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384080,
    "dDurationMs": 5429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see this if we add a second beamsplitter\nto scramble the beams so that we don’t know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389509,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which path brings the photon to detectors\n1 versus 2. Now detector 1 always registers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395919,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a photon and detector 2 never does. Why? Well\nif the photon was traveling both paths at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403639,
    "dDurationMs": 6071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same time, then the combination of phase\nshifts in the beamsplitters causes the photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409710,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction to perfectly line up in detector\n1 - constructive interference, and to perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416560,
    "dDurationMs": 6939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancel out  in detector 2 - destructive interference\nleaving no signal . For that to work this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423499,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair of “possible photons” had to travel\nboth paths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428389,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was called the delayed choice experiment\nbecause, in principle, the second beamsplitter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433179,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be put in place only after the photons\npassed through the first. After they’d made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438039,
    "dDurationMs": 6014,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their decision of a path to take. This experiment,\nand many clever variations of it, have now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444053,
    "dDurationMs": 5206,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been performed. They seem to verify that the\nuniverse really does exist in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449259,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all possible states until the measurement\nis made, and the choice of that measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454159,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be made after the superposition is formed.\nThat means that the reality that emerges depends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460159,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a profound way on the choice of measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463629,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s another experiment. If we measure\nthe spin of one member of a pair of entangled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469539,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles - the choice of the direction of\nthe spin measurement seems to have an instantaneous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475770,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence on its partner, even if that partner is\non the other side of the lab - or on the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480379,
    "dDurationMs": 5211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side of the galaxy. We’ve talked about this\nentanglement stuff in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485590,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it seems that when you interrogate the\nuniverse, the answers that you get depend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490699,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the questions you ask. Wheeler became convinced\nthat that process of interrogation in some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496749,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way creates reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498919,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To help explain what he meant, Wheeler invented\na pathological version of the game 20 questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506169,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the usual game, someone, let’s say Alice,\nthinks of an object, and another person, Bob,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512470,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asks yes/no questions to narrow down the possibilities\nuntil he arrives at only one possible answer:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519130,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the object that Alice had in her mind from\nthe beginning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522190,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Wheeler’s version is a little different.\nHe called it “negative 20 questions,”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528220,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it works like this. Unbeknownst to Bob,\nAlice never thought of an object to begin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532430,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with. Instead, she answers the quest ions\nas they come, maybe completely randomly, maybe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538170,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to some secret pattern. Every answer\nnarrows the space of possible correct answer,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545020,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if Alice still doesn’t know what the\nobject is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548340,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say the rule is that Alice will answer\nyes and no alternately no matter what the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553780,
    "dDurationMs": 7450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question. So Bob asks, “is it smaller than\na bread box?” “yes.” Then “is it living?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561230,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“no.” This line of questioning has narrowed\nBob to the realm of papercl ips and pebbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566760,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But had Bob reversed the order of the questions\nhe’d have interrogated his way towards tigers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573270,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trees and T-rexes. And depending on Alice’s\nrules, you can imagine scenarios where it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579350,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks like Bob’s choice of question influence\nthe past. But it doesn’t, his questions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585330,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are helping reality define itself on the fly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588850,
    "dDurationMs": 7230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler came to the view that the universe\nwas one giant game of negative 20 questions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596080,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the reality we observe is the only\nreality that is consistent with the questions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601860,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asked of it by all observers. The universe,\naccording to Wheeler, was a giant, closed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609500,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“self-excited circuit.” The relationship\nbetween the observer (us) and the observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614990,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(the universe) brought *both* into existence.\nTake this yet further and you have a version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621300,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Strong Anthropic Principle: The only\nreality that can exist is one that can have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626460,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observers whose questions can make that reality\nreal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wheeler called this the “participatory universe.”\nHe symbolized his idea with a sketch of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636520,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giant “U”. On one side of the U is the\nbig bang. As the universe expands, the line"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643030,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the U gets thicker, until we reach the\npresent day, the other side of the U. An eyeball"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647810,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here symbolizes the observer. All the astronomers\nwith their telescopes and observatories are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653380,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aimed back at the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655380,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their observations *now* cause the Big Bang\n*then*. Well, sort of. As Wheeler once wrote,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662030,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“the past has no existence except as it\nis recorded in the present.” Only the self-consistent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668700,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now can have a real existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671660,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As I warned, this all sounds very mystical.\nLet me be clear: Wheeler wasn’t saying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677480,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that conscious minds have direct power over\nreality, as claimed by some of the worst practitioners"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682850,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum woo. But the role of consciousness\nwas something Wheeler really struggled with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689570,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He said “About no feature of “It from\nBit’ do I feel less comfortable than whose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697530,
    "dDurationMs": 7850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit.” After all, information implies a thing\nto KNOW that information. But non-conscious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705380,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entities can, in a sense, know something if\ninformation of that something is recorded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710490,
    "dDurationMs": 8050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside their being somehow. He also once wrote\nthat it was “Not consciousness but the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718540,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distinction between the probe and the probed”\nthat was central to the act of observation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725250,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So “Observation” could well be code for\n“interaction”, in which every time two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729630,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles bump together and become entangled\nwe have an act of measurement. If this all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734250,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems a little vague and wishy washy, it’s\nbecause it is. Wheeler was still obsessively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740410,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thinking and writing about and trying to solidify\nthese ideas until his death in 2008."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747410,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In John Archibald Wheeler’s version of an\ninformational universe, he felt that the information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753360,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the bits - resided in the answers elicited\nby acts of observation. It appears to pull"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759740,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the comforting ground of realism from under\nour feet. But others have taken Wheeler’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideas and put them back on firm ground. It’s\npossible to think of the universe as being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“informational” without altogether giving\nup on realism. In the broad category of “participatory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realism” we have things like quantum bayesianism\nand relational quantum mechanics, in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782680,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe emerges from the information\nthat some set of real entities have about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788050,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other. Crucially, these acknowledge the\nimportance of the observer in defining the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793260,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observed, while still allowing that there’s\nsome kind of substrate to reality. We’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797800,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talk about these ideas soon, in our long quest\nto find that substrate. And do that we just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804310,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to keep asking the right questions of\na participatory space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809860,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get to comments, we\nhave a small favor to ask. We want to check"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814230,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in with all of you to see what episode ideas\nyou have, what new topics you’d like us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explore, and what ideas you’d like us\nto revisit. And in order to organize all this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824680,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great information, we’ve set up a very very\nshort, 8 question survey. We love hearing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your ideas and thoughts and if you have a\nmoment, there’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837320,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be that we live in a participatory\nuniverse that’s self-generated by its observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842630,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or that could be complete nonsense. But I\ncan say with certainty that PBS Space Time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847080,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is generated by participation of its observers\n- that’s you guys - and most especially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853430,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the participation of our wonderful supporters\non Patreon. And today I want to give an extra"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858600,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "special thanks to Mark Evans, who’s supporting\nus at the big bang level. Mark, in creating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865260,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a universe or a show about the universe, every\nlittle bit counts, but there’s nothing bitsy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871470,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor itsy about your generosity. Thanks for\nhelping make it happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877530,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're going to look at your comments\nfrom our two most recent episodes in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881140,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we asked: Where is the center of the universe?\n(if it even has one) and could a universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887220,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually be inside a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890230,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reference to the question “What is the\nuniverse expanding into?”, John Retherford"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895960,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asks whether a fair answer would be “It’s\nexpanding into the future.” I don’t know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902560,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how fair it would be, but I’m going to steal\nit anyway. But it’s at least as fair as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909620,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "saying that the center of the universe and\nthe location of the big bang are points in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913790,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time in the past. So, they’re fair as long\nas you explain what you mean by that - or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920090,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point them to PBS Space Time episode for the\nexplanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924600,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Comrades of Perístanom asks if the geometry\nof the universe - close, open, flat - dictates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931920,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the “temporal shape”. As in does a closed\nuniverse necessarily collapse, and an open"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937870,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one expand forever, and a flat one tend to\nzero expansion. The answer is absolutely yes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944700,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the good old days when we didn’t have\ndark energy. If you just consider the positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949570,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect of gravity, then the spatial geometry\ndictates the final fate. But dark energy screws"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955970,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything up. The presence of dark energy\ntends to flatten the universe because it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960450,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a positive energy density, just like matter.\nBut it’s negative pressure can allow even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965730,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a closed universe to expand forever. Thanks,\ndark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970230,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you asked about the possibility of\ndetecting Hawking radiation in the case that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974190,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe is a black hole. Actually, we\nshould expect a type of Hawking radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979670,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the cosmic event horizon even if the\nuniverse isn’t a black hole. All event horizons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986060,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will lead to a disconnection in the vibrational\nmodes of the quantum fields in a way that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991470,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looks like thermal radiation. That radiation\nhas a wavelength that’s on the scale of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996140,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon. So the horizon radiation\nfrom the cosmic event horizon would have wavelength"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003320,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly the size of the observable universe.\nUnfortunately that makes it not very detectable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008070,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without a universe-sized camera. It would\nalso have such low energy that it wouldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012970,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have any effect on any sane timescale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016970,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Z argues that the universe can't be a black\nhole because the difference between the masses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022520,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our largest black holes in the our universe\nand the whole universe is much too great."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028880,
    "dDurationMs": 3059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would mean for the universe, for the\nuniverse to be a black hole would require"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031939,
    "dDurationMs": 5821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very different physics. In a way that’s\nright. If the universe is a black or a white"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037760,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole then it formed by a different process\nthan the black holes that we’ve observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043079,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our universe. The gap between the masses\nis too enormous for it to just be some extreme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048209,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outlier of the same process. But that’s\nnot the same as saying it requiring different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053179,
    "dDurationMs": 6311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics - general relativity permits white\nor black holes of any size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059490,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way - fun fact - if you calculate the\nsize of a black hole with the mass of our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065690,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observable universe - adding together all\nthe stars, dark matter, other black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071460,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc - then it’s event horizon is the same\nsize as our cosmological even horizon. Sounds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078970,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too much of a coincidence, right? Well actually,\nthis is just another way of saying that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084240,
    "dDurationMs": 6139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is just below the critical mass density\nto avoid recollapse. At least in the absence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090379,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dark energy. Thanks dark energy. That “coincidence”\nitself may require some explanation, but the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097779,
    "dDurationMs": 6671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanation still isn’t that we’re in\na black hole. And it’s not aliens or a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104450,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simulation either. Probably."
    } ]
  } ]
}
